mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c35\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"block\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>\nwhich is <em>not</em> a binomial (it's a trinomial).</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is true. The sum of a binomial and a monomial <em>could be</em> a binomial (see, for example, statement I). </p>\n<p> However, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is <em>not always</em> a binomial (see, for example, statement II). </p>\n<p> So, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is <em>sometimes</em> binomial.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"</p>\n</div></div>",
